      'Indonesia turns out to be one of the easier places on this site to keep your dollars, and the rule everyone worries about does not apply to you. Bank Indonesia Regulation 7 of 2023 is the one that forces exporters to bring proceeds home and park them in the Indonesian financial system, and it is written about goods. Its own definitions say so: export is the activity of taking goods out of the customs area under the customs law, both categories of export proceeds are defined as the proceeds of exporting goods, and an exporter is whoever does that. Selling a service produces no customs declaration, so none of it reaches you, including the three month deadline and the thresholds attached to it. A rule not applying to you is not the same as being allowed, though, so the permission matters separately. The older law the regulation is built on, UU 24 of 1999 on foreign exchange flows, says at Pasal 2 that every resident may freely hold and use foreign exchange. We could not open that statute on a government host, only on a legal text aggregator, so treat it as well corroborated rather than quoted from the gazette. On the product side BCA Dollar takes $100 to open, holds no minimum balance, asks no minimum monthly average and costs $1 a month, and BCA opens it to individuals on the usual documents: Indonesians on a KTP-el plus a tax number or a signed declaration that they have none, foreign residents on a passport plus a KITAS or KITAP residence permit, so a passport alone will not do. Closing it later costs $5. That is cheaper to keep open than the Philippine equivalents, which want $500 sitting in them. Dollars into Indonesia are expensive by the standards of this site: Wise publishes its Indonesia SWIFT fee at $32.90, the second dearest destination on its whole list, against the $35 we assume for a generic wire, so the dollar route saves you almost nothing. The $32.90 is published; the $35 is our assumption, which is why neither row carries our badge. And the spread is deferred rather than escaped, since you pay it whenever you sell those dollars for rupiah. What you buy is the timing, not the spread. Converting through Wise costs a fraction of either dollar route at the sizes we show, so take dollars only if you actually intend to hold them.',
